# Elixir Koans - 13 Functions

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
```

## Functions map arguments to outputs

```elixir
def greet(name) do
    "Hello, #{name}!"
end

assert greet("World") == ___
```

## Single line functions are cool, but mind the comma and the colon!

```elixir
def multiply(a, b), do: a * b

assert 6 == multiply(2, ___)
```

## Functions with the same name are distinguished by the number of arguments they take

```elixir
def first(foo, bar), do: "#{foo} and #{bar}"
def first(foo), do: "Only #{foo}"

assert first("One", "Two") == ___
assert first("One") == ___
```

## Functions can have default argument values

```elixir
def repeat_again(message, times \\ 3) do
  String.duplicate(message, times)
end

assert repeat_again("Hello ") == ___
assert repeat_again("Hello ", 2) == ___
```

## Functions can have guard expressions

```elixir
def sum_up(thing) when is_list(thing), do: :entire_list
def sum_up(_thing), do: :single_thing

assert sum_up([1, 2, 3]) == ___
assert sum_up(1) == ___
```

## Intricate guards are possible, but be mindful of the reader

```elixir
def bigger(a, b) when a > b, do: "#{a} is bigger than #{b}"
def bigger(a, b) when a <= b, do: "#{a} is not bigger than #{b}"

  assert bigger(10, 5) == ___
  assert bigger(4, 27) == ___
```

## For simpler cases, pattern matching is effective

```elixir
def get_number(0), do: "The number was zero"
def get_number(number), do: "The number was #{number}"

assert get_number(0) == ___
assert get_number(5) == ___
```

## Little anonymous functions are common, and called with a dot

```elixir
multiply = fn a, b -> a * b end
assert multiply.(2, 3) == ___
```

## You can even go shorter, by using capture syntax `&()` and positional arguments

```elixir
multiply = &(&1 * &2)
assert multiply.(2, 3) == ___
```

## Prefix a string with & to build a simple anonymous greet function

```elixir
greet = &"Hi, #{&1}!"
assert greet.("Foo") == ___
```

## You can build anonymous functions out of any elixir expression by prefixing it with &

```elixir
three_times = &[&1, &1, &1]
assert three_times.("foo") == ___
```

## You can use pattern matching to define multiple cases for anonymous functions

```elixir
inspirational_quote = fn
  {:ok, result} -> "Success is #{result}"
  {:error, reason} -> "You just lost #{reason}"
end

assert inspirational_quote.({:ok, "no accident"}) == ___
assert inspirational_quote.({:error, "the game"}) == ___
```

## You can pass functions around as arguments. Place an '&' before the name and state the arity

```elixir
def times_five_and_then(number, fun), do: fun.(number * 5)
def square(number), do: number * number

assert times_five_and_then(2, &square/1) == ___
```

## The '&' operation is not needed for anonymous functions

```elixir
cube = fn number -> number * number * number end
assert times_five_and_then(2, cube) == ___
```

## The result of a function can be piped into another function as its first argument

```elixir
result =
  "full-name"
  |> String.split("-")
  |> Enum.map_join(" ", &String.capitalize/1)

assert result == ___
```

## Pipes make data transformation pipelines readable

```elixir
numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

result =
  numbers
  |> Enum.filter(&(&1 > 2))
  |> Enum.map(&(&1 * 2))
  |> Enum.sum()

assert result == ___
```

```elixir
user_input = "  Hello World  "

cleaned =
  user_input
  |> String.trim()
  |> String.downcase()
  |> String.replace(" ", "_")

assert cleaned == ___
```

## Conveniently keyword lists can be used for function options

```elixir
transform = fn str, opts ->
  if opts[:upcase] do
    String.upcase(str)
  else
    str
  end
end

assert transform.("good", upcase: true) == ___
assert transform.("good", upcase: false) == ___
```

## Anonymous functions can use the & capture syntax for very concise definitions

```elixir
add_one = &(&1 + 1)
multiply_by_two = &(&1 * 2)

result = 5 |> add_one.() |> multiply_by_two.()
assert result == ___

# You can also capture existing functions
string_length = &String.length/1
assert string_length.("hello") == ___
```

